    <dashboard enable-sort-zone-taborder='true' name='Dashboard 10'>
      <style />
      <size maxheight='900' maxwidth='1300' minheight='900' minwidth='1300' sizing-mode='fixed' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='4' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone h='98222' id='3' param='horz' type-v2='layout-flow' w='49385' x='50000' y='889'>
            <zone h='98222' id='6' param='vert' type-v2='layout-flow' w='49385' x='50000' y='889'>
              <zone forceUpdate='true' h='97778' id='8' type-v2='text' w='49077' x='50154' y='1111'>
                <formatted-text>
                  <run bold='true' fontalignment='1' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Algerian' fontsize='12' underline='true'>Keep insight:</run>
                  <run fontalignment='1'>Æ&#10;</run>
                  <run fontalignment='1' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>Margin is statistically flat across all three tiers: Gold (20.24%), Platinum (20.22%), Silver (20.13%) a total spread of roughly 0.11 percentage points across the entire customer base. For context, this is a smaller gap than the rounding shown on most dashboard KPIs.</run>
                  <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                  <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>This is a &quot;ruling out&quot; finding, and its value lies precisely in what it eliminates as an explanation. If the Electronics margin problem were concentrated among a specific customer segment  for instance, if high tier, high-spend customers were disproportionately buying discounted Electronics  we&apos;d expect to see tier-level margin vary accordingly. It doesn&apos;t. Every tier generates essentially identical margin per transaction, which means the Electronics margin problem is being distributed evenly across the entire customer base rather than being driven by any one group&apos;s purchasing behavior.</run>
                  <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                  <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>This reframes the appropriate business response. A segment targeted fix  for example, offering fewer discounts specifically to price-sensitive lower-tier customers, or building loyalty incentives aimed at improving Gold tier profitability would not move the needle, because tier was never the variable driving the margin gap in the first place. The lever that matters is category level pricing and discount policy, not customer segmentation.</run>
                  <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                  <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>Real world analogy: this is the equivalent of a restaurant discovering that one specific dish loses money regardless of who orders it  a regular or a first time visitor. The fix isn&apos;t &quot;attract better customers,&quot; it&apos;s &quot;reprice that dish.&quot; Customer Tier remains valuable for loyalty and retention strategy, but this analysis shows it is not the lever for solving the profitability problem identified earlier.</run>
                </formatted-text>
                <zone-style>
                  <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                  <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                  <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                  <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                </zone-style>
              </zone>
              <zone-style>
                <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                <format attr='border-style' value='solid' />
                <format attr='border-width' value='2' />
                <format attr='background-color' value='#eff3e3' />
              </zone-style>
            </zone>
          </zone>
          <zone h='98222' id='5' param='vert' type-v2='layout-flow' w='49385' x='615' y='889'>
            <zone h='98000' id='7' name='Estimated Profit Margin by Customer Tier' w='49231' x='692' y='1000'>
              <layout-cache minheight='238' type-h='scalable' type-w='fixed' />
              <zone-style>
                <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                <format attr='margin' value='4' />
              </zone-style>
            </zone>
            <zone-style>
              <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
              <format attr='border-style' value='solid' />
              <format attr='border-width' value='1' />
              <format attr='background-color' value='#f0f3fa' />
            </zone-style>
          </zone>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='margin' value='8' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
      </zones>
      <devicelayouts>
        <devicelayout auto-generated='true' name='Phone'>
          <size maxheight='700' minheight='700' sizing-mode='vscroll' />
          <zones>
            <zone h='100000' id='24' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
              <zone h='84000' id='23' param='vert' type-v2='layout-flow' w='84000' x='8000' y='8000'>
                <zone fixed-size='280' h='98000' id='7' is-fixed='true' name='Estimated Profit Margin by Customer Tier' w='49231' x='692' y='1000'>
                  <layout-cache minheight='238' type-h='scalable' type-w='fixed' />
                  <zone-style>
                    <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                    <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                    <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                    <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                    <format attr='padding' value='0' />
                  </zone-style>
                </zone>
                <zone forceUpdate='true' h='97778' id='8' type-v2='text' w='49077' x='50154' y='1111'>
                  <formatted-text>
                    <run bold='true' fontalignment='1' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Algerian' fontsize='12' underline='true'>Keep insight:</run>
                    <run fontalignment='1'>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontalignment='1' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>Margin is statistically flat across all three tiers: Gold (20.24%), Platinum (20.22%), Silver (20.13%) a total spread of roughly 0.11 percentage points across the entire customer base. For context, this is a smaller gap than the rounding shown on most dashboard KPIs.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>This is a &quot;ruling out&quot; finding, and its value lies precisely in what it eliminates as an explanation. If the Electronics margin problem were concentrated among a specific customer segment  for instance, if high tier, high-spend customers were disproportionately buying discounted Electronics  we&apos;d expect to see tier-level margin vary accordingly. It doesn&apos;t. Every tier generates essentially identical margin per transaction, which means the Electronics margin problem is being distributed evenly across the entire customer base rather than being driven by any one group&apos;s purchasing behavior.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>This reframes the appropriate business response. A segment targeted fix  for example, offering fewer discounts specifically to price-sensitive lower-tier customers, or building loyalty incentives aimed at improving Gold tier profitability would not move the needle, because tier was never the variable driving the margin gap in the first place. The lever that matters is category level pricing and discount policy, not customer segmentation.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>Real world analogy: this is the equivalent of a restaurant discovering that one specific dish loses money regardless of who orders it  a regular or a first time visitor. The fix isn&apos;t &quot;attract better customers,&quot; it&apos;s &quot;reprice that dish.&quot; Customer Tier remains valuable for loyalty and retention strategy, but this analysis shows it is not the lever for solving the profitability problem identified earlier.</run>
                  </formatted-text>
                  <zone-style>
                    <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                    <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                    <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                    <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                    <format attr='padding' value='0' />
                  </zone-style>
                </zone>
              </zone>
              <zone-style>
                <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                <format attr='margin' value='8' />
