                  <formatted-text>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Deep Insight</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Electronics generates ₹4.26B, approximately 73% of total net revenue, more than six times the next closest category, Home at ₹708.5M. The decline from there isn&apos;t gradual, it&apos;s a cliff. Sports sits at ₹470.8M, Fashion at ₹330M, then a sharp collapse to Grocery, Beauty, and Books, which together represent under 2% of total revenue.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>This pattern points to single category dependency rather than diversified growth. In portfolio terms, a healthy revenue mix typically keeps any single category under roughly 25 to 30% of total revenue, to limit concentration risk. Electronics sits nearly three times that threshold.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Why This Matters</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>A business this reliant on one category is carrying risk that doesn&apos;t show up anywhere in a standard revenue report. Revenue looks strong precisely because Electronics is strong, which can mask how fragile that strength actually is. If anything disrupts Electronics specifically, sharper competitor pricing, a supply chain issue, a cooling in consumer electronics demand, or a regulatory change on imported tech, there&apos;s no real second or third pillar built up to absorb that impact. Total company revenue would take the hit almost directly.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>This also reframes what &quot;success&quot; means so far in this analysis. A category generating 73% of revenue looks like the business&apos;s biggest win. But size alone doesn&apos;t tell you whether that size is healthy, discount driven, or sustainable, that&apos;s exactly what the next few dashboards test.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Key Insights</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Electronics revenue is not just the largest category, it&apos;s disproportionately larger than everything else combined in a meaningful sense, since the bottom three categories barely register.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>The drop from Electronics to Home is a 6x gap, and the drop from Home down to Books is close to 40x, showing the portfolio isn&apos;t gradually tiered, it&apos;s top heavy.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Revenue concentration this extreme usually signals one of two things: either genuine demand strength in Electronics, or reliance on discounting to manufacture that volume, a question this analysis answers directly in the following sections.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                  </formatted-text>
                  <zone-style>
                    <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                    <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
