                <zone fixed-size='862' forceUpdate='true' h='87000' id='13' type-v2='text' w='49333' x='50067' y='9000'>
                  <formatted-text>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12' underline='true'>KEY OBSERVATION:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>This treemap layers stock size against estimated profit margin directly. Nike (37.38 margin, 56,632 units) and Puma (35.16 margin, 54,415 units) are both large and dark blue, the ideal combination of high stock and high profitability. In sharp contrast, Samsung (-4.26), Noise (-4.87), and boAt (-5.07) are sized comparably but colored orange, marking them as loss-making despite meaningful stock investment.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>Why This Matters for Profitability:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>Putting size and color on the same chart makes the disconnect impossible to miss visually, in a way a simple bar chart can&apos;t. A brand can be big (heavily stocked) and still be colored orange (unprofitable), and this chart is the clearest evidence in the entire analysis that inventory allocation is currently blind to margin. If stock decisions followed profitability, orange tiles would be small and blue tiles would be large. Instead, several orange tiles are just as large as the best performing blue ones.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>Key Insights:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Calibri' fontsize='12'>Tile size shows what the business is investing in, tile color shows what&apos;s actually paying off, and right now those two signals don&apos;t agree for several Electronics brands.</run>
